WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.288
At three in the afternoon, the boss approaches from the other end of the corridor.

2
00:00:03.788 --> 00:00:07.055
A whole row of screens switches back to spreadsheets at once.

3
00:00:07.555 --> 00:00:11.806
Once the boss enters the elevator, someone stretches and the break room comes alive again.

4
00:00:12.706 --> 00:00:16.924
Sociologist Erving Goffman called these front and back regions.

5
00:00:17.424 --> 00:00:24.980
In his 1959 book, he wrote that in front of others, we highlight some things and suppress what might undermine the impression.

6
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:30.730
What is highlighted appears front stage; what is suppressed emerges backstage.

7
00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:36.248
Backstage, performers can relax, drop their front and stop reciting their lines.

8
00:00:37.148 --> 00:00:44.356
He quoted a shipyard account: hearing the foreman was coming, workers found things to do—even tightening an already-tight bolt again.

9
00:00:45.256 --> 00:00:49.178
He also described the kitchen of a hotel in Scotland’s Shetland Islands in detail.

10
00:00:49.678 --> 00:00:55.213
Butter guests had used and pressed out of shape was reshaped in the kitchen and served again.

11
00:00:55.713 --> 00:01:05.653
The waitresses wanted the kitchen door open to carry plates; the manager wanted it shut to hide the kitchen. Almost every day, it was slammed shut and pushed open again.

12
00:01:06.553 --> 00:01:13.016
Remember, this is an observation of one hotel over seventy years ago, not an experiment, with no statistical data.

13
00:01:13.516 --> 00:01:17.667
He did not define backstage as the real you. It is backstage relative to a particular performance.

14
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:23.249
By that definition, in another setting the break room could be the front stage of another performance.

15
00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:26.491
Where is your backstage? Share your thoughts.

16
00:01:26.991 --> 00:01:28.859
Leave yourself a little backstage space.
